//! core/fs — the filesystem: the two effects, and the doors onto them.
//!
//! Every function here names `FsRead` or `FsWrite` in its bounds, so the disk
//! is visible in the signature — and *which half* of the disk is visible too.
//!
//! ## Reading and writing are two authorities
//!
//! `FsRead` is four methods and `FsWrite` is eight, and they are two effects
//! rather than one because they are two grants. A program that reads its
//! configuration has not thereby earned the right to delete it, and the
//! difference is one a reader of a context should be able to see:
//!
//! ```buri sig
//! # from "core/effect" import { Alloc, IoError };
//! # from "core/fs" import * as fs;
//! # from "core/fs" import { FsRead, Path };
//! #
//! export fn settings<C: Alloc + FsRead>(ctx: C, at: Path): Result<Str, IoError> {
//!     fs.readText(ctx, at)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! That signature is a promise the compiler keeps: nothing `settings` calls
//! can write, because nothing it could hand `ctx` to can ask for `FsWrite`
//! from a context that does not bind it.
//!
//! The cost is that a program doing both binds both — `FsRead: host.fs,
//! FsWrite: host.fs`, one filesystem under two authorities — and that is
//! deliberate rather than a shortcoming waiting for a language feature. It is
//! also what makes a *decorator* cheap: a fake or a fault injector that only
//! has to be read from restates four methods rather than twelve.
//!
//! ## Every operation takes a `Path`
//!
//! Not a `Str`. `core/path` normalizes text once, at the boundary where it
//! becomes a path, so a separator too many is a compile-time conversation
//! rather than a `.NotFound` that reads exactly like a missing file. See that
//! module for what normalizing does and, more importantly, what it does not.
//!
//! ## Directories
//!
//! `makeDir` creates one and every missing parent of it; `removeDir` removes
//! one that is **empty**. Neither is recursive, and the asymmetry is only
//! apparent: creating a parent nobody asked for leaves a directory where a
//! directory was wanted, and deleting a child nobody named deletes a file
//! somebody wrote. So the creation is `mkdir -p` and the removal is `rmdir`,
//! and a program that wants a recursive delete writes the walk out of `listDir`,
//! `remove` and `removeDir` — where every path it deletes is one it named.
//!
//! ## Text and octets
//!
//! `readText` and `writeText` are the text pair; `readBytes` and `writeBytes`
//! are the same two operations without an encoding step. Reading text
//! substitutes U+FFFD for what is not valid UTF-8, so a file that is not text
//! has to be read as octets to come back unchanged.
//!
//! ## What is atomic, and what is durable
//!
//! Two different promises, and a program that keeps a write-ahead log needs
//! both stated rather than assumed.
//!
//! **Atomic** is `rename`, and only `rename`: after it returns, the
//! destination names either the file it named before or the file the source
//! named, never a mixture and never nothing. Within one filesystem this is the
//! operating system's own guarantee — POSIX `rename(2)`, which the JavaScript
//! runner reaches through `fs.renameSync`. Across two filesystems there is no
//! such operation, so the answer is `.Err(.CrossDevice)` rather than a copy
//! that pretends.
//!
//! **Durable** is `sync`, and only `sync`. `writeBytes` and `append` return
//! when the platform accepted the octets, which is not the same as the storage
//! device holding them. What each backend can promise:
//!
//! | Backend | `sync` is | Survives |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | native, Linux | `fsync(2)` | the process, and the operating system |
//! | native, macOS | `fcntl(F_FULLFSYNC)` | those two, and the drive's own cache |
//! | JavaScript runner | `fs.fsyncSync` | the process, and the operating system |
//! | `core/host/testing`'s `TestFs` | nothing to flush | the test that wrote it |
//!
//! The row that is easy to read past is macOS on the JavaScript runner: node's
//! `fsync` is the plain `fsync(2)`, which on macOS asks the drive to write its
//! cache and does not wait for it. The native backend asks for `F_FULLFSYNC`
//! there, which does. Neither is wrong; they are different promises, and a
//! program whose commit point must survive a power cut is entitled to know
//! which one it has.
//!
//! `sync` on a **directory** flushes the directory entry rather than a file's
//! contents, which is what makes a preceding `rename` durable rather than
//! merely visible. The checkpoint sequence is therefore four calls —
//! `writeBytes` the temporary, `sync` it, `rename` it over the old one, `sync`
//! the directory holding both — and [`writeAtomic`] is those four written once.

from "core/effect" import { Alloc, IoError };
from "core/path" import { Path };

from "core/effect" export { IoError };

from "core/path" export { Path };

/// Looking at what is on the filesystem.
///
/// The half of the filesystem that changes nothing. A context binding this and
/// not `FsWrite` grants a program the authority to read and no authority at
/// all to write, which is a sentence the type system will hold anyone to.
///
/// Declared here rather than in `core/effect` because every method names
/// `Path`, and `core/path` names `Alloc` — so the declarations live on the
/// side of that dependency where they can say what they mean.
export effect FsRead {
    fn readFile(self, path: Path): Result<Str, IoError>;
    fn fileExists(self, path: Path): Bool;
    fn readDir(self, path: Path): Result<[Str], IoError>;

    /// The octets, unchanged. `readFile` decodes UTF-8 and substitutes U+FFFD
    /// for what is not; this is the operation for a file that is not text.
    fn readFileBytes(self, path: Path): Result<[U8], IoError>;
}

/// Changing what is on the filesystem.
///
/// Eight methods, and none of them reads: a decorator over this restates these
/// eight, and a program that only writes — a logger, a recorder — binds this
/// and nothing else.
export effect FsWrite {
    fn writeFile(self, path: Path, body: Str): Result<(), IoError>;
    /// Truncates an existing file, and creates one that is absent.
    fn writeFileBytes(self, path: Path, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;
    /// Appends the whole body, creating the file when it is absent.
    fn appendFile(self, path: Path, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;
    /// Replaces `destination`, atomically within one filesystem.
    fn renameFile(self, source: Path, destination: Path): Result<(), IoError>;
    fn removeFile(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError>;
    /// Removes the directory, which must be **empty**. `.Err(.Other)` where it
    /// is not, and `.Err(.NotFound)` where the path names nothing.
    ///
    /// Empty and not recursive, deliberately: the recursive version behind a
    /// one-word name is a mistake that only happens once, and `removeDir`
    /// below states the whole rule. It is the operation that lets a program
    /// that made a scratch directory leave the filesystem as it found it —
    /// `makeDir` had no inverse at all before it.
    fn removeDir(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError>;
    /// Parents included; an existing directory is `.Ok`.
    fn makeDir(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError>;
    /// The commit point: what was written to `path` is durable when this
    /// returns. `sync` below states what each platform can promise.
    fn syncFile(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError>;
}

export fn readText<C: Alloc + FsRead>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<Str, IoError> {
    ctx.readFile(path)
}

export fn writeText<C: Alloc + FsWrite>(
    ctx: C,
    path: Path,
    body: Str,
): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.writeFile(path, body)
}

export fn exists<C: FsRead>(ctx: C, path: Path): Bool {
    ctx.fileExists(path)
}

export fn listDir<C: Alloc + FsRead>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<[Str], IoError> {
    ctx.readDir(path)
}

/// The file's octets, unchanged.
///
/// `.Err(.NotFound)` where the path names nothing, exactly as `readText` does.
/// The difference from `readText` is only the encoding step: this is what a
/// file holding a protobuf message or a log record wants, and `readText` would
/// substitute U+FFFD into it.
export fn readBytes<C: Alloc + FsRead>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<[U8], IoError> {
    ctx.readFileBytes(path)
}

/// The file's octets, or `.None` where the path names nothing.
///
/// "Read it, or carry on without it" — a cache, an optional override, a
/// checkpoint that may not have been written yet. The difference between this
/// and `readBytes` is only which of the two answers is an error, and the
/// difference between this and `exists` followed by `readBytes` is that this
/// one is *one* call: a file can be deleted between the two, so the two-call
/// form has a window in which it reports a file that is there as missing, or
/// fails on one it just saw. The `.None` therefore comes from matching
/// `.NotFound` on the read itself and from nowhere else.
///
/// Every other failure is still a failure. A file that is there and cannot be
/// read is `.Err(.PermissionDenied)`, not `.Ok(.None)` — "absent" and
/// "forbidden" are different facts and a caller that folded them together
/// would retry the wrong one.
///
/// ```buri run
/// from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Stdout };
/// from "core/fs" import * as fs;
/// from "core/fs" import { FsRead };
/// from "core/host" import * as host;
/// from "core/io" import * as io;
/// from "core/path" import * as path;
///
/// export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
///     let ctx = context {
///         Alloc: host.alloc,
///         FsRead: host.fs,
///         Stdout: host.stdout,
///     };
///     let answer = match (fs.readBytesIfExists(ctx, path.of(ctx, "no-such-file"))) {
///         .Ok(.None) => "absent",
///         .Ok(.Some(_body)) => "there",
///         .Err(_e) => "unreadable",
///     };
///     let _ = io.println(ctx, "${answer}").ignore();
///     .Ok(())
/// }
/// ```
///
/// ```stdout
/// absent
/// ```
export fn readBytesIfExists<C: Alloc + FsRead>(
    ctx: C,
    path: Path,
): Result<Option<[U8]>, IoError> {
    match (ctx.readFileBytes(path)) {
        .Ok(body) => .Ok(.Some(body)),
        .Err(.NotFound) => .Ok(.None),
        .Err(other) => .Err(other),
    }
}

/// Replaces the file's contents with these octets, creating it when it is
/// absent.
///
/// Durable only after `sync`; see the module header.
export fn writeBytes<C: Alloc + FsWrite>(
    ctx: C,
    path: Path,
    body: [U8],
): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.writeFileBytes(path, body)
}

/// Replaces the file's contents with these octets, or leaves the file exactly
/// as it was.
///
/// The checkpoint sequence the module header spells out, written once: a
/// sibling temporary is written and synced, renamed over the destination, and
/// the directory holding both is synced. A reader of the destination sees the
/// old contents or the new ones, never a mixture and never nothing — including
/// across a crash, which is what the two syncs are for and what a plain
/// `writeBytes` cannot promise.
///
/// **The temporary is `<path>.tmp`, a sibling.** Beside the destination rather
/// than in a scratch directory, because `rename` is atomic only within one
/// filesystem and a sibling is the one place guaranteed to be on the same one.
/// Two writers of one path therefore share the temporary and the last rename
/// wins — the same answer they would get writing directly, except that no
/// reader ever sees half of either.
///
/// The first failure is the answer, and the temporary is left where it is: a
/// `writeAtomic` that failed cleaning up after itself would report the cleanup
/// rather than the write.
///
/// `.Err(.Other)` where the path has no last component — the root, and `.` —
/// because there is no sibling to write beside it.
export fn writeAtomic<C: Alloc + FsWrite>(
    ctx: C,
    path: Path,
    body: [U8],
): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (path.withSuffix(ctx, ".tmp")) {
        .None => .Err(.Other("a path with no file name cannot be written atomically")),
        .Some(temp) => {
            let _written = ctx.writeFileBytes(temp, body)?;
            let _flushed = ctx.syncFile(temp)?;
            let _moved = ctx.renameFile(temp, path)?;
            match (path.parent()) {
                .None => .Ok(()),
                .Some(directory) => ctx.syncFile(directory),
            }
        },
    }
}

/// Adds these octets to the end of the file, creating it when it is absent.
///
/// Whole-body, so one call is one record and there is no partial-append window
/// to reason about. The position is taken and the octets written as one
/// operation, so two writers appending to one log interleave records rather
/// than overwriting each other's.
///
/// Durable only after `sync`, which is the whole reason a log has a commit
/// point rather than a write.
export fn append<C: Alloc + FsWrite>(
    ctx: C,
    path: Path,
    body: [U8],
): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.appendFile(path, body)
}

/// Moves `source` onto `destination`, replacing it if it is there.
///
/// Atomic within one filesystem: a reader of the destination sees the old file
/// or the new one and never a partial write, which is what makes "write a
/// temporary, then rename it over the real one" a crash-safe checkpoint.
/// `.Err(.CrossDevice)` where the two paths are not on one filesystem, so the
/// case where atomicity is unavailable is a value to match on rather than a
/// footnote to remember.
///
/// The rename is *visible* when this returns and *durable* after `sync` of the
/// directory holding it. `writeAtomic` is the whole sequence.
export fn rename<C: FsWrite>(
    ctx: C,
    source: Path,
    destination: Path,
): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.renameFile(source, destination)
}

/// Deletes the file.
///
/// `.Err(.NotFound)` where the path names nothing: the most common way for a
/// deletion to be wrong is a path that was never right, and a `remove` that
/// answered `.Ok` to it would hide that. "Delete it if it is there" is
/// `result.ignore(fs.remove(ctx, p))`, written where it is meant.
///
/// Files only. `removeDir` is the directory half, and it is a second name
/// rather than a second behaviour of this one: a recursive delete behind a
/// one-word name is a mistake that only happens once, so neither of them is
/// recursive and the one that removes a directory says so in its name.
export fn remove<C: FsWrite>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.removeFile(path)
}

/// Deletes the directory, which must be **empty**.
///
/// This is `makeDir`'s inverse, and it is the one the effect went without: a
/// program that made a scratch directory per run had no way to leave the
/// filesystem as it found it, so a long-lived checkout accumulated one empty
/// directory per run ever made.
///
/// **Empty, and not recursive.** `rmdir(2)` is what this is, on every backend
/// that has a real filesystem, and a directory that still holds something is
/// `.Err(.Other)` — the platform's own `ENOTEMPTY`, carrying the platform's own
/// sentence. There is deliberately no recursive form and no flag that turns one
/// on: a recursive delete is a program-sized decision about which files may go,
/// and a program that wants one writes the walk itself out of `listDir` and
/// these two removals, where every path it deletes is one it named.
///
/// The other two answers are the ones `remove` gives, for the same reasons:
/// `.Err(.NotFound)` where the path names nothing, because a removal of what
/// was never there is a belief that was wrong; and `.Err(.NotADirectory)`
/// where the path names a file, because that request cannot be satisfied by
/// anybody. "Remove it if it is there" is `fs.removeDir(ctx, p).ignore()`,
/// written where it is meant.
export fn removeDir<C: FsWrite>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.removeDir(path)
}

/// Creates the directory, and every parent of it that is missing.
///
/// An existing directory is `.Ok`, so a tenant's directory is one call rather
/// than a check and a race — the two callers who both find it missing both
/// succeed. A path that already names a *file* is `.Err(.AlreadyExists)`,
/// because that request cannot be satisfied by anybody.
///
/// The asymmetry with `remove` is deliberate. `makeDir` states a postcondition
/// — this directory exists — that somebody else reaching it first does not
/// violate. `remove` names a file the caller believes is there, and a path
/// naming nothing means that belief was wrong.
export fn makeDir<C: FsWrite>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.makeDir(path)
}

/// Waits until what was written to `path` is durable.
///
/// This is a program's commit point, stated as one. `writeBytes`, `append` and
/// `rename` return when the platform accepted the operation; they survive a
/// power cut only after this returns. On a directory path it flushes the
/// directory entry, which is what a preceding `rename` needs.
///
/// The module header has the table of what each backend can promise — they are
/// not all the same promise, and the macOS rows differ between the two.
export fn sync<C: FsWrite>(ctx: C, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
    ctx.syncFile(path)
}
